OSKit UNIX Server — AF_UNIX Sockets
====================================

Minix 3-style socket server for AF_UNIX. Mirrors `inet.lsysl`'s
shape but everything stays inside one server (no NIC or wire
format) — the unix server is a self-contained registry of
path-keyed slots that pair up via send/recv queues.

Session 1 of the AF_UNIX arc: SOCK_DGRAM (sendto/recvfrom over
a path) and SOCK_STREAM (listen/accept/connect/send/recv).

Session 2 adds SCM_RIGHTS fd-passing for DGRAM sockets via the
new SENDMSG/RECVMSG commands. The owner check for every command
is now multi-tid — each slot keeps an `owners[UNIX_MAX_OWNERS]`
ring plus `nrefs_in_queues` so a slot stays alive across
sendmsg-then-recvmsg even if all original owners close in
between. STREAM SCM_RIGHTS is deferred (no per-byte ancillary
plumbing yet); transferring non-AF_UNIX fds is also deferred
(the wire format only carries unix-server slot indices).

Path namespace
--------------

Slix doesn't tie AF_UNIX paths to the filesystem (no inode
bookkeeping in TFS for sockets, no mknod). Paths are stored
verbatim as opaque keys inside the server's slot table:
binding sets `bound_path[]` + `path_len`; connect/sendto walks
the table looking for a matching entry. This matches Linux
"abstract namespace" semantics (a leading NUL on the path)
without the namespace prefix — every AF_UNIX path on slix
behaves abstractly.

Wire protocol
-------------

All messages start with a 1-byte command code. Replies start
with a 1-byte status (0 on success, -errno on failure). Paths
travel as `path_len(1)` + `path_bytes(N)` where N ≤
UNIX_PATH_MAX. Multi-byte integers are big-endian.

- SOCKET (1)    req: cmd(1) + stype(1)
                rep: status(1) + fd(1)
- BIND (2)      req: cmd(1) + fd(1) + plen(1) + path(N)
                rep: status(1)
- CONNECT (3)   req: cmd(1) + fd(1) + plen(1) + path(N)
                rep: status(1)
- LISTEN (4)    req: cmd(1) + fd(1) + backlog(1)
                rep: status(1)
- ACCEPT (5)    req: cmd(1) + fd(1)
                rep: status(1) + new_fd(1) + plen(1) + path(N)
                where path is the connector's bound_path
                (empty if connector wasn't bind()ed first)
- SENDTO (6)    req: cmd(1) + fd(1) + plen(1) + path(N) + dlen_be(2) + data(M)
                rep: status(1) + nsent_be(2)
- RECVFROM (7)  req: cmd(1) + fd(1) + maxlen_be(2)
                rep: status(1) + plen(1) + path(N) + dlen_be(2) + data(M)
- SEND (8)      req: cmd(1) + fd(1) + dlen_be(2) + data(M)
                rep: status(1) + nsent_be(2)
- RECV (9)      req: cmd(1) + fd(1) + maxlen_be(2)
                rep: status(1) + dlen_be(2) + data(M)
- CLOSE (10)    req: cmd(1) + fd(1)
                rep: status(1)
- PID_EXIT (11) req: cmd(1) + tid_be(4)
                rep: status(1)
- SENDMSG (12)  req: cmd(1) + fd(1) + plen(1) + path(N) +
                     nfds(1) + slots(F) + dlen_be(2) + data(M)
                rep: status(1) + nsent_be(2)
- RECVMSG (13)  req: cmd(1) + fd(1) + max_nfds(1) + maxlen_be(2)
                rep: status(1) + plen(1) + path(N) +
                     nfds(1) + slots(F) + dlen_be(2) + data(M)
- ACCEPT_NB (14)   same wire as ACCEPT — returns -EAGAIN when no
                   pending connection (never parks).
- RECVFROM_NB (15) same wire as RECVFROM — returns -EAGAIN on
                   empty queue (never parks).
- RECV_NB (16)     same wire as RECV — returns -EAGAIN if rx_buf
                   empty *and* peer is still alive. EOF (peer
                   closed, no data) still returns 0.
- POLL (17)     req: cmd(1) + fd(1)
                rep: status(1) + mask_be(2) + fire_seq_le(4)
                Readiness query only — no parking. Used by
                shim's `epoll_query_ready` for `ppoll` /
                `select` / `epoll` integration.
- EPOLL_SUB (18)   req: cmd(1) + fd(1) + epoll_inst(1)
                   rep: status(1)
                   Subscribes (sender_tid, fd, epoll_inst).
                   Idempotent.
- EPOLL_UNSUB (19) req: cmd(1) + fd(1) + epoll_inst(1)
                   rep: status(1)
                   Drops the matching sub.
- EPOLL_INST_CLOSE (20)
                   req: cmd(1) + epoll_inst(1)
                   rep: status(1)
                   Drops every sub belonging to (sender_tid,
                   epoll_inst) — bulk cleanup when an epoll
                   fd closes.
- RECVMSG_NB (21)  same wire as RECVMSG — returns -EAGAIN on
                   empty queue (never parks). RECVMSG itself
                   parks by default (POSIX-conformant
                   blocking recvmsg).

The `_NB` variants are used by the shim when the fd is in
`O_NONBLOCK` mode (or when `MSG_DONTWAIT` is passed in the
flags). They share their parking-variant's handler — the
shim writes a different `cmd` byte and the handler skips the
park step.

All handlers enforce per-fd ownership: the caller's tid must
appear in the slot's `owners[]` ring. The ring supports up
to `UNIX_MAX_OWNERS` simultaneous owners so SCM_RIGHTS
fd-passing (Session 2) can grant a co-owner without losing
the original. `nrefs_in_queues` counts pending fd-grant
queue entries that hold the slot alive while in transit.

    module oskit.servers

    import oskit.ipc.{port_create, port_register, ipc_recv, ipc_reply}
    import oskit.services.{rs_ready, notify_send_to}


Protocol command codes (byte value of buf[0]).

    const UNIX_CMD_SOCKET    = 1
    const UNIX_CMD_BIND      = 2
    const UNIX_CMD_CONNECT   = 3
    const UNIX_CMD_LISTEN    = 4
    const UNIX_CMD_ACCEPT    = 5
    const UNIX_CMD_SENDTO    = 6
    const UNIX_CMD_RECVFROM  = 7
    const UNIX_CMD_SEND      = 8
    const UNIX_CMD_RECV      = 9
    const UNIX_CMD_CLOSE     = 10
    const UNIX_CMD_PID_EXIT  = 11
    const UNIX_CMD_SENDMSG   = 12
    const UNIX_CMD_RECVMSG   = 13
    const UNIX_CMD_ACCEPT_NB        = 14
    const UNIX_CMD_RECVFROM_NB      = 15
    const UNIX_CMD_RECV_NB          = 16
    const UNIX_CMD_POLL             = 17
    const UNIX_CMD_EPOLL_SUB        = 18
    const UNIX_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB      = 19
    const UNIX_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE = 20
    const UNIX_CMD_RECVMSG_NB       = 21
    const UNIX_CMD_GETSOCKNAME      = 22
    const UNIX_CMD_GETPEERNAME      = 23
    const UNIX_CMD_SOCKETPAIR       = 24
    const UNIX_CMD_SHUTDOWN         = 25

EPOLL/POLL bit values match `POSIX_EPOLL*` 1:1 (POLLIN=0x001,
POLLOUT=0x004, POLLERR=0x008, POLLHUP=0x010). The shim's
`epoll_query_ready` masks the reply against the caller's
want set + EPOLLERR + EPOLLHUP.

    const UNIX_EPOLLIN  = 0x001
    const UNIX_EPOLLOUT = 0x004
    const UNIX_EPOLLERR = 0x008
    const UNIX_EPOLLHUP = 0x010

Socket types — match Linux `SOCK_STREAM=1`, `SOCK_DGRAM=2`.

    const UNIX_SOCK_STREAM = 1
    const UNIX_SOCK_DGRAM  = 2

Errno values returned in `status` byte. Sysl signed-byte cast
preserves the negative value across the wire.

    const UNIX_EAGAIN      = 11
    const UNIX_EINVAL      = 22
    const UNIX_EBADF       = 9
    const UNIX_EADDRINUSE  = 98
    const UNIX_ENOENT      = 2
    const UNIX_EPIPE       = 32
    const UNIX_ECONNREFUSED = 111
    const UNIX_ENOTCONN    = 107
    const UNIX_ENOMEM      = 12

Capacity constants. Sized down from inet for a first pass —
AF_UNIX paths are usually small messages anyway. Path 108
matches the POSIX `sockaddr_un.sun_path[108]`.

    const UNIX_MAX_SOCKETS = 16
    const UNIX_PATH_MAX    = 108
    const UNIX_DG_QUEUE    = 4
    const UNIX_DG_DATA_MAX = 256
    const UNIX_RX_BUF      = 2048
    const UNIX_BACKLOG_MAX = 8
    const UNIX_MAX_OWNERS  = 4
    const UNIX_SCM_MAX_FDS = 4

Server-side IPC buffer. Max wire request is SENDTO with full
DGRAM payload: cmd(1) + fd(1) + plen(1) + path(108) + dlen(2) +
data(256) = 369 bytes. Round up to 512 for headroom.

    const UNIX_MSG_BUF = 512


Datagram queue entry. `src_path` records the sender's bound
path (or empty string for unbound senders) so recvfrom can
return it to the caller via the standard sockaddr_un round-trip.

    struct UnixDgram
        in_use: int
        src_path: [108]byte
        src_path_len: int
        data: [256]byte
        data_len: int
        nfds: int
        fds: [4]int


Per-slot state. The same struct serves DGRAM and STREAM
sockets — fields that aren't relevant to one mode just stay
unused. Memory cost: 16 × (108 + 4 × 372 + 2048 + 8 × ints +
108) ≈ 65 KB total — fits well within a server's address
space.

`peer_idx` is the connected partner's slot for STREAM; -1
means unconnected (or peer has closed). For listeners it's
unused.

`pending_q` is the listener's accept-backlog: slot indices of
new server-side sockets allocated by connect() that haven't
been accepted yet.

`pending_recv_tid` parks a blocked recv/recvfrom — set when
the queue is empty and the caller wanted blocking behavior;
cleared (and the caller unblocked via ipc_reply) when the
matching send/sendto wakes the slot.

`pending_accept_tid` does the same for blocked accept().

    struct UnixSocket
        in_use: int
        owners: [4]int
        owners_count: int
        nrefs_in_queues: int
        stype: int
        bound: int
        bound_path: [108]byte
        path_len: int
        listening: int
        peer_idx: int
        peer_closed: int
        rx_buf: [2048]byte
        rx_head: int
        rx_tail: int
        rx_count: int
        dg_queue: [4]UnixDgram
        dg_head: int
        dg_tail: int
        dg_count: int
        pending_q: [8]int
        pending_head: int
        pending_tail: int
        pending_count: int
        pending_recv_tid: int
        pending_recv_maxlen: int
        pending_recv_peek: int
        pending_recv_is_msg: int
        pending_recv_max_nfds: int
        pending_accept_tid: int
        connector_path: [108]byte
        connector_path_len: int
        fire_seq: int
        shut_wr: int
        shut_rd: int

    var unix_sockets: [16]UnixSocket
    var unix_port = -1


Epoll subscriber table. Each entry pins a (owner_tid, fd,
epoll_inst) tuple so any readiness edge on that fd posts a
wake to the owner. 16 entries is enough for the working
set we expect — every AF_UNIX fd watched by a typical event
loop holds one row.

    const UNIX_MAX_EPOLL_SUBS = 16

    struct UnixEpollSub
        in_use: int
        unix_fd: int
        owner_tid: int
        epoll_inst: int

    var unix_epoll_subs: [16]UnixEpollSub

    unix_epoll_init_subs()
        var i = 0
        while i < UNIX_MAX_EPOLL_SUBS
            unix_epoll_subs[i].in_use = 0
            i += 1


    unix_epoll_alloc_sub() -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < UNIX_MAX_EPOLL_SUBS
            if unix_epoll_subs[i].in_use == 0
                return i
            i += 1
        -1


    unix_epoll_find_sub(unix_fd: int, owner_tid: int, inst: int) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < UNIX_MAX_EPOLL_SUBS
            if unix_epoll_subs[i].in_use != 0
                if unix_epoll_subs[i].unix_fd == unix_fd
                    if unix_epoll_subs[i].owner_tid == owner_tid
                        if unix_epoll_subs[i].epoll_inst == inst
                            return i
            i += 1
        -1


Drop every subscription belonging to a particular
(owner_tid, epoll_inst) pair. Called by EPOLL_INST_CLOSE.

    unix_epoll_drop_inst(owner_tid: int, inst: int)
        var i = 0
        while i < UNIX_MAX_EPOLL_SUBS
            if unix_epoll_subs[i].in_use != 0
                if unix_epoll_subs[i].owner_tid == owner_tid
                    if unix_epoll_subs[i].epoll_inst == inst
                        unix_epoll_subs[i].in_use = 0
            i += 1


Drop every subscription belonging to a particular owner_tid.
Called from PID_EXIT so a process that died without closing
its epoll fds doesn't leak rows.

    unix_epoll_drop_owner(owner_tid: int)
        var i = 0
        while i < UNIX_MAX_EPOLL_SUBS
            if unix_epoll_subs[i].in_use != 0
                if unix_epoll_subs[i].owner_tid == owner_tid
                    unix_epoll_subs[i].in_use = 0
            i += 1


Big-endian helpers.

    unix_read_u16(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        ((int(p[off]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff)

    unix_write_u16(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte((v >> 8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte(v & 0xff)

    unix_read_u32(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        ((int(p[off]) & 0xff) << 24) | ((int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(p[off + 2]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(p[off + 3]) & 0xff)


Initialize the slot table. Called once from the server main
loop before `port_register`.

    unix_init_sockets()
        var i = 0
        while i < UNIX_MAX_SOCKETS
            unix_sockets[i].in_use = 0
            unix_sockets[i].owners_count = 0
            unix_sockets[i].nrefs_in_queues = 0
            unix_sockets[i].bound = 0
            unix_sockets[i].listening = 0
            unix_sockets[i].peer_idx = -1
            unix_sockets[i].peer_closed = 0
            unix_sockets[i].path_len = 0
            unix_sockets[i].rx_head = 0
            unix_sockets[i].rx_tail = 0
            unix_sockets[i].rx_count = 0
            unix_sockets[i].dg_head = 0
            unix_sockets[i].dg_tail = 0
            unix_sockets[i].dg_count = 0
            unix_sockets[i].pending_head = 0
            unix_sockets[i].pending_tail = 0
            unix_sockets[i].pending_count = 0
            unix_sockets[i].pending_recv_tid = -1
            unix_sockets[i].pending_recv_peek = 0
            unix_sockets[i].pending_recv_is_msg = 0
            unix_sockets[i].pending_recv_max_nfds = 0
            unix_sockets[i].pending_accept_tid = -1
            unix_sockets[i].connector_path_len = 0
            unix_sockets[i].fire_seq = 0
            unix_sockets[i].shut_wr = 0
            unix_sockets[i].shut_rd = 0
            var j = 0
            while j < UNIX_MAX_OWNERS
                unix_sockets[i].owners[j] = -1
                j += 1
            j = 0
            while j < UNIX_DG_QUEUE
                unix_sockets[i].dg_queue[j].in_use = 0
                j += 1
            i += 1


Owners-ring helpers. The ring is a flat array of up to
`UNIX_MAX_OWNERS` tids; the live entries occupy the prefix
`[0..owners_count)` and -1 elsewhere. Lookup / add / remove
are O(UNIX_MAX_OWNERS), which is fine at n=4.

    unix_slot_has_owner(idx: int, tid: int) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < unix_sockets[idx].owners_count
            if unix_sockets[idx].owners[i] == tid
                return 1
            i += 1
        0

    unix_slot_add_owner(idx: int, tid: int) -> int
        if unix_slot_has_owner(idx, tid) != 0
            return 1
        if unix_sockets[idx].owners_count >= UNIX_MAX_OWNERS
            return 0
        unix_sockets[idx].owners[unix_sockets[idx].owners_count] = tid
        unix_sockets[idx].owners_count += 1
        1


Bump the per-slot `fire_seq` counter so the shim's per-fd
EPOLLET edge tracker can detect "this specific fd crossed a
readiness boundary" without a per-instance bulk clear of
`last_reported`. Then walk the epoll subscriber table and
post a wake to every (owner_tid) subscribed to this slot,
so a blocking `epoll_wait` can return without polling.

Called at every state change that affects EPOLLIN/OUT/HUP —
sendto/sendmsg enqueue, recvfrom drain, send to peer, recv
drain, listen-backlog push, accept drain, peer close.

    unix_bump_fire_seq(idx: int)
        if idx >= 0 && idx < UNIX_MAX_SOCKETS
            if unix_sockets[idx].in_use != 0
                unix_sockets[idx].fire_seq = unix_sockets[idx].fire_seq + 1
                var i = 0
                while i < UNIX_MAX_EPOLL_SUBS
                    if unix_epoll_subs[i].in_use != 0
                        if unix_epoll_subs[i].unix_fd == idx
                            notify_send_to(unix_epoll_subs[i].owner_tid, 1)
                    i += 1

Remove `tid` from the owners ring; compact the prefix so
`owners[0..owners_count)` stays contiguous. No-op if the tid
isn't present.

    unix_slot_remove_owner(idx: int, tid: int)
        var i = 0
        while i < unix_sockets[idx].owners_count
            if unix_sockets[idx].owners[i] == tid
                var j = i
                while j < unix_sockets[idx].owners_count - 1
                    unix_sockets[idx].owners[j] = unix_sockets[idx].owners[j + 1]
                    j += 1
                unix_sockets[idx].owners_count -= 1
                unix_sockets[idx].owners[unix_sockets[idx].owners_count] = -1
                return
            i += 1


Validate `fd` belongs to `sender`. Same shape as
`inet_fd_valid`: rejects out-of-range, free, or
foreign-owner slots. Multi-owner-aware — accepts any tid in
the slot's owners ring.

    unix_fd_valid(fd: int, sender: int) -> int
        if fd < 0
            return 0
        if fd >= UNIX_MAX_SOCKETS
            return 0
        if unix_sockets[fd].in_use == 0
            return 0
        if unix_slot_has_owner(fd, sender) == 0
            return 0
        1


Compare slot `idx`'s bound_path against an external buffer.
Avoids taking the address of an inner struct array field — sysl
doesn't accept `&struct.array_field[0]` in expression position.

    unix_slot_path_eq(idx: int, path: *byte, plen: int) -> int
        if unix_sockets[idx].path_len != plen
            return 0
        var i = 0
        while i < plen
            if unix_sockets[idx].bound_path[i] != path[i]
                return 0
            i += 1
        1


Find a slot whose `bound_path` matches the given key. Returns
index or -1. Linear scan — UNIX_MAX_SOCKETS is small enough
that hashing isn't worth the bookkeeping at this scale.

    unix_find_bound(path: *byte, plen: int) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < UNIX_MAX_SOCKETS
            if unix_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if unix_sockets[i].bound != 0
                    if unix_slot_path_eq(i, path, plen) != 0
                        return i
            i += 1
        -1


Allocate a fresh slot, initialize default fields, return idx
or -1 if the table is full.

    unix_alloc_slot(owner_tid: int, stype: int) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < UNIX_MAX_SOCKETS
            if unix_sockets[i].in_use == 0
                unix_sockets[i].in_use = 1
                unix_sockets[i].stype = stype
                unix_sockets[i].bound = 0
                unix_sockets[i].listening = 0
                unix_sockets[i].peer_idx = -1
                unix_sockets[i].peer_closed = 0
                unix_sockets[i].path_len = 0
                unix_sockets[i].rx_head = 0
                unix_sockets[i].rx_tail = 0
                unix_sockets[i].rx_count = 0
                unix_sockets[i].dg_head = 0
                unix_sockets[i].dg_tail = 0
                unix_sockets[i].dg_count = 0
                unix_sockets[i].pending_head = 0
                unix_sockets[i].pending_tail = 0
                unix_sockets[i].pending_count = 0
                unix_sockets[i].pending_recv_tid = -1
                unix_sockets[i].pending_recv_peek = 0
                unix_sockets[i].pending_recv_is_msg = 0
                unix_sockets[i].pending_recv_max_nfds = 0
                unix_sockets[i].pending_accept_tid = -1
                unix_sockets[i].nrefs_in_queues = 0
                unix_sockets[i].connector_path_len = 0
                unix_sockets[i].fire_seq = 0
                unix_sockets[i].shut_wr = 0
                unix_sockets[i].shut_rd = 0
                var j = 0
                while j < UNIX_MAX_OWNERS
                    unix_sockets[i].owners[j] = -1
                    j += 1
                unix_sockets[i].owners[0] = owner_tid
                unix_sockets[i].owners_count = 1
                j = 0
                while j < UNIX_DG_QUEUE
                    unix_sockets[i].dg_queue[j].in_use = 0
                    j += 1
                return i
            i += 1
        -1


Free a slot. Caller is responsible for waking any pending
peer. We scrub `bound` so a future bind to the same path
succeeds; the rest of the state is reset on re-allocation.

    unix_free_slot(idx: int)
        unix_sockets[idx].in_use = 0
        unix_sockets[idx].owners_count = 0
        unix_sockets[idx].nrefs_in_queues = 0
        unix_sockets[idx].bound = 0
        unix_sockets[idx].listening = 0
        unix_sockets[idx].peer_idx = -1
        unix_sockets[idx].peer_closed = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < UNIX_MAX_OWNERS
            unix_sockets[idx].owners[i] = -1
            i += 1


Last-owner cleanup. Called from `unix_handle_close` and
`unix_handle_pid_exit` when removing the last owner of a
slot drops `owners_count` to 0 and there are no in-flight
queue references (`nrefs_in_queues == 0`). Walks the same
peer-EOF / backlog-flush / parked-accept-EBADF dance the
single-owner close used to do, then frees the slot.

    unix_full_close(idx: int)
        val peer = unix_sockets[idx].peer_idx
        if peer >= 0
            if unix_sockets[peer].in_use != 0
                unix_sockets[peer].peer_closed = 1
                unix_sockets[peer].peer_idx = -1
                unix_bump_fire_seq(peer)
                val parked = unix_sockets[peer].pending_recv_tid
                if parked >= 0
                    unix_sockets[peer].pending_recv_tid = -1
                    var pr: [3]byte
                    pr[0] = 0
                    pr[1] = 0
                    pr[2] = 0
                    ipc_reply(parked, pr, 3)

        if unix_sockets[idx].listening != 0
            while unix_sockets[idx].pending_count > 0
                val head = unix_sockets[idx].pending_head
                val pending = unix_sockets[idx].pending_q[head]
                unix_sockets[idx].pending_head = (head + 1) % UNIX_BACKLOG_MAX
                unix_sockets[idx].pending_count -= 1
                if unix_sockets[pending].in_use != 0
                    val ppeer = unix_sockets[pending].peer_idx
                    if ppeer >= 0
                        if unix_sockets[ppeer].in_use != 0
                            unix_sockets[ppeer].peer_closed = 1
                            unix_sockets[ppeer].peer_idx = -1
                            unix_bump_fire_seq(ppeer)
                    unix_free_slot(pending)

        if unix_sockets[idx].pending_accept_tid >= 0
            val waiter = unix_sockets[idx].pending_accept_tid
            unix_sockets[idx].pending_accept_tid = -1
            var ar: [3]byte
            ar[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            ar[1] = 0
            ar[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(waiter, ar, 3)

        unix_free_slot(idx)


Append `n` bytes from `src` to slot `idx`'s rx ring. Returns
bytes actually written (capped by free space). The ring is
2 KB; long writes get truncated and the caller chunks via
upstream send loops.

    unix_rx_append(idx: int, src: *byte, n: int) -> int
        val free_space = UNIX_RX_BUF - unix_sockets[idx].rx_count
        var to_copy = n
        if to_copy > free_space
            to_copy = free_space
        if to_copy <= 0
            return 0
        var i = 0
        var t = unix_sockets[idx].rx_tail
        while i < to_copy
            unix_sockets[idx].rx_buf[t] = src[i]
            t = (t + 1) % UNIX_RX_BUF
            i += 1
        unix_sockets[idx].rx_tail = t
        unix_sockets[idx].rx_count += to_copy
        unix_bump_fire_seq(idx)
        to_copy


Drain up to `maxlen` bytes from slot `idx`'s rx ring into
`dst`. Returns bytes actually read. If `peek` is non-zero,
the bytes are copied without advancing the read cursor.

    unix_rx_drain(idx: int, dst: *byte, maxlen: int, peek: int) -> int
        var to_copy = unix_sockets[idx].rx_count
        if to_copy > maxlen
            to_copy = maxlen
        if to_copy <= 0
            return 0
        var i = 0
        var h = unix_sockets[idx].rx_head
        while i < to_copy
            dst[i] = unix_sockets[idx].rx_buf[h]
            h = (h + 1) % UNIX_RX_BUF
            i += 1
        if peek == 0
            unix_sockets[idx].rx_head = h
            unix_sockets[idx].rx_count -= to_copy
            unix_bump_fire_seq(idx)
        to_copy


Enqueue a datagram on `dst_idx`'s dgram queue, sourced from
`src_idx`'s bound_path. Caller has already verified queue
isn't full. Truncates payloads larger than UNIX_DG_DATA_MAX
silently — POSIX permits truncation on oversized datagrams,
the caller sees a smaller `nsent`.

    unix_dg_enqueue(dst_idx: int, src_idx: int, data: *byte, dlen: int) -> int
        val slot = unix_sockets[dst_idx].dg_tail
        unix_sockets[dst_idx].dg_queue[slot].in_use = 1
        // Default to no ancillary fds; SENDMSG overrides after this
        // call. Without this clear, a queue slot reused after a
        // previous SCM_RIGHTS send would carry stale fds into a plain
        // sendto's payload.
        unix_sockets[dst_idx].dg_queue[slot].nfds = 0
        var sp = unix_sockets[src_idx].path_len
        if sp > UNIX_PATH_MAX
            sp = UNIX_PATH_MAX
        unix_sockets[dst_idx].dg_queue[slot].src_path_len = sp
        var i = 0
        while i < sp
            unix_sockets[dst_idx].dg_queue[slot].src_path[i] = unix_sockets[src_idx].bound_path[i]
            i += 1
        var dl = dlen
        if dl > UNIX_DG_DATA_MAX
            dl = UNIX_DG_DATA_MAX
        unix_sockets[dst_idx].dg_queue[slot].data_len = dl
        i = 0
        while i < dl
            unix_sockets[dst_idx].dg_queue[slot].data[i] = data[i]
            i += 1
        unix_sockets[dst_idx].dg_tail = (slot + 1) % UNIX_DG_QUEUE
        unix_sockets[dst_idx].dg_count += 1
        unix_bump_fire_seq(dst_idx)
        dl


Drain one datagram from slot `idx`'s queue. Writes the
recvfrom-shaped reply into `out_reply` (the caller's IPC
reply buffer): status(1) + plen(1) + path(N) + dlen_be(2) +
data(M). Returns total reply length.

    unix_dg_dequeue_reply(idx: int, maxlen: int, peek: int,
            out_reply: *byte) -> int
        val slot = unix_sockets[idx].dg_head
        out_reply[0] = byte(0)
        val sp = unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].src_path_len
        out_reply[1] = byte(sp & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < sp
            out_reply[2 + i] = unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].src_path[i]
            i += 1
        var dl = unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].data_len
        if dl > maxlen
            dl = maxlen
        unix_write_u16(out_reply, 2 + sp, dl)
        i = 0
        while i < dl
            out_reply[2 + sp + 2 + i] = unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].data[i]
            i += 1

If this is a real drain (not a peek), release any cmsg fd
queue-refs the entry was carrying and orphan-free their
slots if they fall to zero owners + zero queue refs.
recvfrom doesn't carry ancillary, so the fds are silently
dropped — but their nrefs_in_queues counter MUST be
decremented or the slots will leak. The recvmsg path does
this in its own dispatch.

        if peek == 0
            val nfds_drop = unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].nfds
            var k = 0
            while k < nfds_drop
                val sidx = unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].fds[k]
                unix_sockets[sidx].nrefs_in_queues -= 1
                if unix_sockets[sidx].owners_count == 0
                    if unix_sockets[sidx].nrefs_in_queues == 0
                        if unix_sockets[sidx].in_use != 0
                            unix_full_close(sidx)
                k += 1
            unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].nfds = 0
            unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].in_use = 0
            unix_sockets[idx].dg_head = (slot + 1) % UNIX_DG_QUEUE
            unix_sockets[idx].dg_count -= 1
            unix_bump_fire_seq(idx)
        2 + sp + 2 + dl


### SOCKET handler

    unix_handle_socket(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val stype = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if stype != UNIX_SOCK_STREAM
            if stype != UNIX_SOCK_DGRAM
                var err: [2]byte
                err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
                err[1] = 0
                ipc_reply(sender, err, 2)
                return
        val idx = unix_alloc_slot(sender, stype)
        if idx < 0
            var err: [2]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ENOMEM)
            err[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 2)
            return
        var reply: [2]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte(idx & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)


### BIND handler

Sets the path on an existing slot. Path must be 1..UNIX_PATH_MAX
bytes and not already taken by another bound slot.

    unix_handle_bind(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        val plen = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        if plen <= 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if plen > UNIX_PATH_MAX
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].bound != 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if unix_find_bound(buf + 3, plen) >= 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EADDRINUSE)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        var i = 0
        while i < plen
            unix_sockets[fd].bound_path[i] = (buf + 3)[i]
            i += 1
        unix_sockets[fd].path_len = plen
        unix_sockets[fd].bound = 1
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### LISTEN handler

Marks a STREAM slot as listening. Backlog is clamped to the
server's UNIX_BACKLOG_MAX. POSIX requires a prior bind, so
we enforce that.

    unix_handle_listen(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].stype != UNIX_SOCK_STREAM
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].bound == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        unix_sockets[fd].listening = 1
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### CONNECT handler (STREAM + DGRAM)

For STREAM: allocates a server-end slot owned by the
listener's owner, pairs it with the connector, enqueues it on
the listener's backlog. If a thread is already blocked on
accept(), unparks it. Reply to the connector is immediate (no
pending state) — matches loopback-AF_UNIX semantics where
connect succeeds the moment the new socket is queued.

For DGRAM: just looks up the target by path and sets
`peer_idx` so subsequent `send(2)` / `write(2)` route there
without an explicit destination. No backlog, no listening
requirement — Linux DGRAM connect is purely a default-peer
record. Re-connecting overwrites the previous peer.

    unix_handle_connect(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        val plen = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        if plen <= 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        val target = unix_find_bound(buf + 3, plen)
        if target < 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ECONNREFUSED)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return

DGRAM short-circuit: target must also be DGRAM, then just
set peer_idx and ack.

        if unix_sockets[fd].stype == UNIX_SOCK_DGRAM
            if unix_sockets[target].stype != UNIX_SOCK_DGRAM
                var err: [1]byte
                err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ECONNREFUSED)
                ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
                return
            unix_sockets[fd].peer_idx = target
            var ok: [1]byte
            ok[0] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, ok, 1)
            return

        if unix_sockets[fd].stype != UNIX_SOCK_STREAM
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].peer_idx >= 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if unix_sockets[target].listening == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ECONNREFUSED)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if unix_sockets[target].pending_count >= UNIX_BACKLOG_MAX
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ECONNREFUSED)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return

Allocate the server-end slot; it is owned by the listener's
owner so accept() on that thread can claim it and ops on it
pass `unix_fd_valid`. peer_idx links the two halves so
subsequent send/recv route through each other's `rx_buf`.

If the connector itself was bound to a path (typical Linux
servers don't bind clients, but some apps do for naming /
auth), copy it onto the server-end as `connector_path` so the
accept reply can hand it back. Unbound connectors leave
`connector_path_len = 0` and accept reports an empty path.

        val server_end = unix_alloc_slot(unix_sockets[target].owners[0],
            UNIX_SOCK_STREAM)
        if server_end < 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ENOMEM)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        unix_sockets[fd].peer_idx = server_end
        unix_sockets[server_end].peer_idx = fd
        if unix_sockets[fd].bound != 0
            val cplen = unix_sockets[fd].path_len
            var ci = 0
            while ci < cplen
                unix_sockets[server_end].connector_path[ci] = unix_sockets[fd].bound_path[ci]
                ci += 1
            unix_sockets[server_end].connector_path_len = cplen
        else
            unix_sockets[server_end].connector_path_len = 0

Linux's `getsockname` on the accepted server end returns the
listening socket's path. Copy the listener's bound_path into
the server_end without setting `bound=1` — `unix_find_bound`
filters by `bound`, so this won't create a lookup alias.

        val lplen = unix_sockets[target].path_len
        var li = 0
        while li < lplen
            unix_sockets[server_end].bound_path[li] = unix_sockets[target].bound_path[li]
            li += 1
        unix_sockets[server_end].path_len = lplen

Reply to the connector first so the connect() unblocks
without depending on listener-side state. If a thread was
already parked on accept(), unblock it now with the new fd —
otherwise enqueue the server_end on the backlog for a
future accept(). The accept reply is variable-length:
`status(1) + new_fd(1) + plen(1) + path(P)`.

        var creply: [1]byte
        creply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, creply, 1)

        if unix_sockets[target].pending_accept_tid >= 0
            val waiter = unix_sockets[target].pending_accept_tid
            unix_sockets[target].pending_accept_tid = -1
            var ar: [128]byte
            ar[0] = 0
            ar[1] = byte(server_end & 0xff)
            val plen = unix_sockets[server_end].connector_path_len
            ar[2] = byte(plen & 0xff)
            var pi = 0
            while pi < plen
                ar[3 + pi] = unix_sockets[server_end].connector_path[pi]
                pi += 1
            ipc_reply(waiter, &ar[0], 3 + plen)
        else
            val tail = unix_sockets[target].pending_tail
            unix_sockets[target].pending_q[tail] = server_end
            unix_sockets[target].pending_tail = (tail + 1) % UNIX_BACKLOG_MAX
            unix_sockets[target].pending_count += 1
            unix_bump_fire_seq(target)


### ACCEPT handler

Drains one slot from the listener's backlog. If the queue is
empty, parks the caller via `pending_accept_tid` — the
matching CONNECT will unblock it. The `_NB` cmd byte
(UNIX_CMD_ACCEPT_NB) sets `no_park=1`, which makes an empty
queue reply with -EAGAIN immediately instead of parking.

Reply wire shape: `status(1) + new_fd(1) + plen(1) + path(P)`.
Errors emit `(-errno, 0, 0)` — 3 bytes; the shim peeks status
first, so the trailing zero plen is harmless.

    unix_handle_accept(sender: int, buf: *byte, no_park: int)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].listening == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].pending_count > 0
            val head = unix_sockets[fd].pending_head
            val server_end = unix_sockets[fd].pending_q[head]
            unix_sockets[fd].pending_head = (head + 1) % UNIX_BACKLOG_MAX
            unix_sockets[fd].pending_count -= 1
            unix_bump_fire_seq(fd)
            var reply: [128]byte
            reply[0] = 0
            reply[1] = byte(server_end & 0xff)
            val plen = unix_sockets[server_end].connector_path_len
            reply[2] = byte(plen & 0xff)
            var pi = 0
            while pi < plen
                reply[3 + pi] = unix_sockets[server_end].connector_path[pi]
                pi += 1
            ipc_reply(sender, &reply[0], 3 + plen)
            return
        if no_park != 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EAGAIN)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_accept_tid = sender


### SENDTO handler (DGRAM)

Locates the bound destination, copies the message into its
queue. If the queue is full, fails with -EAGAIN — POSIX
allows blocking but the existing inet pattern returns
status=1 + nsent=0 to signal "drop". For unix we emit
explicit -EAGAIN since the shim is the source of blocking
semantics.

If a thread is parked on recvfrom for the destination, drain
the just-enqueued datagram and reply directly.

    unix_handle_sendto(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].stype != UNIX_SOCK_DGRAM
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val plen = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        val dlen = unix_read_u16(buf, 3 + plen)
        val data: *byte = buf + 3 + plen + 2

`plen == 0` is the "no destination" form used by socketpair-
DGRAM and by `send(2)` / `write(2)` on a connected DGRAM
socket: route to `peer_idx` instead of doing a path lookup.

        var target = -1
        if plen == 0
            val pi = unix_sockets[fd].peer_idx
            if pi < 0
                var err: [3]byte
                err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ENOTCONN)
                err[1] = 0
                err[2] = 0
                ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
                return
            target = pi
        else
            target = unix_find_bound(buf + 3, plen)
        if target < 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ECONNREFUSED)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        if unix_sockets[target].stype != UNIX_SOCK_DGRAM
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ECONNREFUSED)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return

If the receiver is parked and its queue is empty,
short-circuit straight into the parked reply — saves a queue
hop and means the wake-up happens before the sender's reply,
so a tight ping-pong sees the pong already on the wire when
ping() returns. The reply shape depends on whether the parker
is recvfrom (recvfrom-shape) or recvmsg (recvmsg-shape with
nfds=0 — sendto carries no ancillary).

        val parked = unix_sockets[target].pending_recv_tid
        if parked >= 0
            if unix_sockets[target].dg_count == 0
                val is_msg = unix_sockets[target].pending_recv_is_msg
                unix_sockets[target].pending_recv_tid = -1
                unix_sockets[target].pending_recv_is_msg = 0
                val maxlen = unix_sockets[target].pending_recv_maxlen
                var dl = dlen
                if dl > UNIX_DG_DATA_MAX
                    dl = UNIX_DG_DATA_MAX
                val spl = unix_sockets[fd].path_len
                var dlcap = dl
                if dlcap > maxlen
                    dlcap = maxlen
                if is_msg != 0
                    var pr: [600]byte
                    pr[0] = 0
                    pr[1] = byte(spl & 0xff)
                    var i = 0
                    while i < spl
                        pr[2 + i] = unix_sockets[fd].bound_path[i]
                        i += 1
                    pr[2 + spl] = byte(0)
                    unix_write_u16(&pr[0], 2 + spl + 1, dlcap)
                    i = 0
                    while i < dlcap
                        pr[2 + spl + 1 + 2 + i] = data[i]
                        i += 1
                    ipc_reply(parked, &pr[0], 2 + spl + 1 + 2 + dlcap)
                else
                    var pr: [512]byte
                    pr[0] = 0
                    pr[1] = byte(spl & 0xff)
                    var i = 0
                    while i < spl
                        pr[2 + i] = unix_sockets[fd].bound_path[i]
                        i += 1
                    unix_write_u16(&pr[0], 2 + spl, dlcap)
                    i = 0
                    while i < dlcap
                        pr[2 + spl + 2 + i] = data[i]
                        i += 1
                    ipc_reply(parked, &pr[0], 2 + spl + 2 + dlcap)
                var sr: [3]byte
                sr[0] = 0
                unix_write_u16(&sr[0], 1, dl)
                ipc_reply(sender, sr, 3)
                return

Otherwise enqueue and reply with the count.

        if unix_sockets[target].dg_count >= UNIX_DG_QUEUE
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EAGAIN)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val nsent = unix_dg_enqueue(target, fd, data, dlen)
        var sreply: [3]byte
        sreply[0] = 0
        unix_write_u16(&sreply[0], 1, nsent)
        ipc_reply(sender, sreply, 3)


### RECVFROM handler (DGRAM)

Drains one datagram and replies. If empty, parks the caller
via `pending_recv_tid`. The `_NB` cmd byte
(UNIX_CMD_RECVFROM_NB) sets `no_park=1`, which makes an empty
queue reply with -EAGAIN immediately.

    unix_handle_recvfrom(sender: int, buf: *byte, no_park: int)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [4]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            err[3] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 4)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].stype != UNIX_SOCK_DGRAM
            var err: [4]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            err[3] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 4)
            return
        val maxlen = unix_read_u16(buf, 2)
        if unix_sockets[fd].dg_count > 0
            var pr: [512]byte
            val n = unix_dg_dequeue_reply(fd, maxlen, 0, &pr[0])
            ipc_reply(sender, &pr[0], n)
            return
        if no_park != 0
            var err: [4]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EAGAIN)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            err[3] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 4)
            return
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_tid = sender
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_maxlen = maxlen
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_peek = 0
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_is_msg = 0


### SEND handler (STREAM)

Appends to the peer's rx_buf. If the peer has a parked recv,
unblocks it. If the peer is gone (`peer_closed`), returns
-EPIPE.

    unix_handle_send(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].stype != UNIX_SOCK_STREAM
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val peer = unix_sockets[fd].peer_idx
        if peer < 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ENOTCONN)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].peer_closed != 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EPIPE)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].shut_wr != 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EPIPE)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val dlen = unix_read_u16(buf, 2)
        val data: *byte = buf + 4
        val nsent = unix_rx_append(peer, data, dlen)
        var sreply: [3]byte
        sreply[0] = 0
        unix_write_u16(&sreply[0], 1, nsent)
        ipc_reply(sender, sreply, 3)

If the peer is parked on recv, drain and unblock.

        val parked = unix_sockets[peer].pending_recv_tid
        if parked >= 0
            unix_sockets[peer].pending_recv_tid = -1
            val maxlen = unix_sockets[peer].pending_recv_maxlen
            var pr: [2050]byte
            pr[0] = 0
            val n = unix_rx_drain(peer, &pr[3], maxlen, 0)
            unix_write_u16(&pr[0], 1, n)
            ipc_reply(parked, &pr[0], 3 + n)


### RECV handler (STREAM)

Drains rx_buf and replies. If empty and peer is gone,
returns 0 bytes (clean EOF). If empty and peer is alive,
parks the caller. The `_NB` cmd byte (UNIX_CMD_RECV_NB) sets
`no_park=1`, which turns the alive-peer empty case into
-EAGAIN. EOF (peer closed, no data) still returns 0 — that's
how POSIX distinguishes "would block" from "stream closed".

    unix_handle_recv(sender: int, buf: *byte, no_park: int)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].stype != UNIX_SOCK_STREAM
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val maxlen = unix_read_u16(buf, 2)
        if unix_sockets[fd].shut_rd != 0
            var pr: [3]byte
            pr[0] = 0
            unix_write_u16(&pr[0], 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, pr, 3)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].rx_count > 0
            var pr: [2050]byte
            pr[0] = 0
            val n = unix_rx_drain(fd, &pr[3], maxlen, 0)
            unix_write_u16(&pr[0], 1, n)
            ipc_reply(sender, &pr[0], 3 + n)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].peer_idx < 0
            var pr: [3]byte
            pr[0] = 0
            unix_write_u16(&pr[0], 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, pr, 3)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].peer_closed != 0
            var pr: [3]byte
            pr[0] = 0
            unix_write_u16(&pr[0], 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, pr, 3)
            return
        val peer_for_eof = unix_sockets[fd].peer_idx
        if unix_sockets[peer_for_eof].shut_wr != 0
            var pr: [3]byte
            pr[0] = 0
            unix_write_u16(&pr[0], 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, pr, 3)
            return
        if no_park != 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EAGAIN)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_tid = sender
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_maxlen = maxlen
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_peek = 0
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_is_msg = 0


### SENDMSG handler (DGRAM, SCM_RIGHTS)

Bundles an in-band datagram with up to `UNIX_SCM_MAX_FDS`
ancillary unix-socket fds. The caller passes a list of
slot indices it owns; the server validates ownership,
increments each slot's `nrefs_in_queues`, and enqueues the
datagram + fd payload onto the destination's `dg_queue`.
The receiver claims co-ownership in `unix_handle_recvmsg`.

Wire format mirrors SENDTO with a single ancillary block
slotted between the path and the payload:
`cmd(1) + fd(1) + plen(1) + path(P) + nfds(1) + slots(F)
+ dlen_be(2) + data(D)`.

Limitations: DGRAM only (STREAM SCM_RIGHTS deferred);
fd-passing is restricted to AF_UNIX socket fds (transferring
TCP/UDP/file fds is a separate Session-3 concern). After
the enqueue, if a thread is parked on `recvfrom` for the
same target, this handler drains the head of queue and
wakes it — the cmsg fds in that drained entry are silently
dropped because recvfrom doesn't carry ancillary, but their
queue-refs are released by `unix_dg_dequeue_reply`.

    unix_handle_sendmsg(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].stype != UNIX_SOCK_DGRAM
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val plen = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        val nfds = int(buf[3 + plen]) & 0xff
        if nfds > UNIX_SCM_MAX_FDS
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val slots_off = 3 + plen + 1
        val dlen = unix_read_u16(buf, slots_off + nfds)
        val data: *byte = buf + slots_off + nfds + 2
        val target = unix_find_bound(buf + 3, plen)
        if target < 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ECONNREFUSED)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        if unix_sockets[target].stype != UNIX_SOCK_DGRAM
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ECONNREFUSED)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return

Validate every fd up front — if any is bogus, reject the
whole sendmsg without partial transfer. Each slot must be
in range, in_use, and in the sender's owners ring.

        var k = 0
        while k < nfds
            val sidx = int(buf[slots_off + k]) & 0xff
            if sidx < 0
                var err: [3]byte
                err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
                err[1] = 0
                err[2] = 0
                ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
                return
            if sidx >= UNIX_MAX_SOCKETS
                var err: [3]byte
                err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
                err[1] = 0
                err[2] = 0
                ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
                return
            if unix_sockets[sidx].in_use == 0
                var err: [3]byte
                err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
                err[1] = 0
                err[2] = 0
                ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
                return
            if unix_slot_has_owner(sidx, sender) == 0
                var err: [3]byte
                err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
                err[1] = 0
                err[2] = 0
                ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
                return
            k += 1

        if unix_sockets[target].dg_count >= UNIX_DG_QUEUE
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EAGAIN)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return

Enqueue the datagram, copy the fd slot indices into the
ancillary slot, and bump each transferred slot's queue-ref
counter so the underlying socket stays alive even if all
its current owners close before the receiver drains.

        val nsent = unix_dg_enqueue(target, fd, data, dlen)
        val q = (unix_sockets[target].dg_tail + UNIX_DG_QUEUE - 1) % UNIX_DG_QUEUE
        unix_sockets[target].dg_queue[q].nfds = nfds
        k = 0
        while k < nfds
            val sidx = int(buf[slots_off + k]) & 0xff
            unix_sockets[target].dg_queue[q].fds[k] = sidx
            unix_sockets[sidx].nrefs_in_queues += 1
            k += 1
        var sreply: [3]byte
        sreply[0] = 0
        unix_write_u16(&sreply[0], 1, nsent)
        ipc_reply(sender, sreply, 3)

If a thread is parked on recvfrom or recvmsg for `target`,
drain the head of queue (the entry we just enqueued) and
reply to it. The reply shape depends on the parker:
recvfrom drops the cmsg fds (queue-refs released by
`unix_dg_dequeue_reply`); recvmsg goes through the
recvmsg-drain helper which transfers fd ownership.

        val parked = unix_sockets[target].pending_recv_tid
        if parked >= 0
            val pmaxlen = unix_sockets[target].pending_recv_maxlen
            val pmsg = unix_sockets[target].pending_recv_is_msg
            val pmaxn = unix_sockets[target].pending_recv_max_nfds
            unix_sockets[target].pending_recv_tid = -1
            unix_sockets[target].pending_recv_is_msg = 0
            if pmsg != 0
                unix_dg_drain_for_msg(target, parked, pmaxn, pmaxlen)
            else
                var pr: [512]byte
                val n = unix_dg_dequeue_reply(target, pmaxlen, 0, &pr[0])
                ipc_reply(parked, &pr[0], n)


### RECVMSG handler (DGRAM, SCM_RIGHTS)

Drains one queued datagram. For each ancillary fd in the
entry, releases its queue-ref and grants co-ownership to
the recvmsg caller. If the receiver's `max_nfds` budget is
smaller than the queued count, excess fds are dropped (the
queue-ref is released, but no co-ownership granted — those
slots become orphaned candidates for free).

If the queue is empty, returns -EAGAIN. No parking — a
caller wanting blocking semantics should use `recvfrom`.
Recvmsg parking is deferred to a later session.

Wire reply: `status(1) + src_plen(1) + src_path(P) +
nfds(1) + slots(F) + dlen_be(2) + data(D)`.

Drain helper for the recvmsg-shaped reply. Used both by
`unix_handle_recvmsg`'s direct dequeue and by sendto /
sendmsg wakes when the parker is a recvmsg (not recvfrom).
Builds the reply, transfers fd ownership for `min(queued,
max_nfds)` slots, releases queue-refs for *all* slots, and
advances the head pointer.

    unix_dg_drain_for_msg(idx: int, receiver: int, max_nfds: int,
            maxlen: int)
        val slot = unix_sockets[idx].dg_head
        val sp = unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].src_path_len
        val queued_nfds = unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].nfds
        var grant = queued_nfds
        if grant > max_nfds
            grant = max_nfds
        var pr: [600]byte
        pr[0] = 0
        pr[1] = byte(sp & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < sp
            pr[2 + i] = unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].src_path[i]
            i += 1
        pr[2 + sp] = byte(grant & 0xff)
        i = 0
        while i < grant
            val sidx = unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].fds[i]
            pr[2 + sp + 1 + i] = byte(sidx & 0xff)
            i += 1
        var dl = unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].data_len
        if dl > maxlen
            dl = maxlen
        unix_write_u16(&pr[0], 2 + sp + 1 + grant, dl)
        i = 0
        while i < dl
            pr[2 + sp + 1 + grant + 2 + i] = unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].data[i]
            i += 1
        i = 0
        while i < queued_nfds
            val sidx = unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].fds[i]
            unix_sockets[sidx].nrefs_in_queues -= 1
            if i < grant
                unix_slot_add_owner(sidx, receiver)
            if unix_sockets[sidx].owners_count == 0
                if unix_sockets[sidx].nrefs_in_queues == 0
                    if unix_sockets[sidx].in_use != 0
                        unix_full_close(sidx)
            i += 1
        unix_sockets[idx].dg_queue[slot].in_use = 0
        unix_sockets[idx].dg_head = (slot + 1) % UNIX_DG_QUEUE
        unix_sockets[idx].dg_count -= 1
        unix_bump_fire_seq(idx)
        ipc_reply(receiver, &pr[0], 2 + sp + 1 + grant + 2 + dl)


    unix_handle_recvmsg(sender: int, buf: *byte, no_park: int)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [4]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            err[3] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 4)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].stype != UNIX_SOCK_DGRAM
            var err: [4]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            err[3] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 4)
            return
        val max_nfds_raw = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        var max_nfds = max_nfds_raw
        if max_nfds > UNIX_SCM_MAX_FDS
            max_nfds = UNIX_SCM_MAX_FDS
        val maxlen = unix_read_u16(buf, 3)
        if unix_sockets[fd].dg_count > 0
            unix_dg_drain_for_msg(fd, sender, max_nfds, maxlen)
            return
        if no_park != 0
            var err: [4]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EAGAIN)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            err[3] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 4)
            return
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_tid = sender
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_maxlen = maxlen
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_peek = 0
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_is_msg = 1
        unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_max_nfds = max_nfds


### POLL handler

Computes a readiness mask for the slot. EPOLLIN fires on:
DGRAM with queued datagrams; STREAM with rx_buf bytes ready
or peer-closed (so the next recv returns 0/EOF without
blocking); listener with at least one pending connection.
EPOLLOUT is always set — slix doesn't enforce send-side
backpressure on STREAM, and DGRAM `sendto` returns -EAGAIN
synchronously when the destination's queue is full (so
"writable" is meaningless at the source-fd granularity).
EPOLLHUP fires on a STREAM that has lost its peer.

Reply layout: `status(1) + mask_be(2) + fire_seq_le(4)`.
The fire_seq counter advances on every readiness edge so the
shim's per-fd EPOLLET edge tracker can detect "this specific
fd crossed a boundary since last scan" without bulk-clearing
sibling entries' last_reported state.

    unix_handle_poll(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [7]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            var i = 1
            while i < 7
                err[i] = 0
                i += 1
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 7)
            return
        var mask = UNIX_EPOLLOUT
        if unix_sockets[fd].stype == UNIX_SOCK_DGRAM
            if unix_sockets[fd].dg_count > 0
                mask = mask | UNIX_EPOLLIN
        else
            if unix_sockets[fd].listening != 0
                if unix_sockets[fd].pending_count > 0
                    mask = mask | UNIX_EPOLLIN
            else
                if unix_sockets[fd].rx_count > 0
                    mask = mask | UNIX_EPOLLIN
                if unix_sockets[fd].peer_closed != 0
                    mask = mask | UNIX_EPOLLIN
                    mask = mask | UNIX_EPOLLHUP
                if unix_sockets[fd].shut_rd != 0
                    mask = mask | UNIX_EPOLLIN
                val ppi = unix_sockets[fd].peer_idx
                if ppi >= 0
                    if unix_sockets[ppi].shut_wr != 0
                        mask = mask | UNIX_EPOLLIN
        val seq = unix_sockets[fd].fire_seq
        var reply: [7]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte((mask >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[2] = byte(mask & 0xff)
        reply[3] = byte(seq & 0xff)
        reply[4] = byte((seq >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[5] = byte((seq >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[6] = byte((seq >> 24) & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)


### EPOLL_SUB / UNSUB / INST_CLOSE handlers

SUB upserts: if the same (sender_tid, unix_fd, epoll_inst)
triple is already in the table, no-op success. The shim
calls SUB on every CTL_ADD and CTL_MOD; the events mask isn't
shipped because the unix server's job is just "wake on edge",
not filter — the shim's epoll_query_ready handles filtering.

    unix_handle_epoll_sub(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val inst = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        var reply: [1]byte
        if fd < 0 || fd >= UNIX_MAX_SOCKETS
            reply[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val existing = unix_epoll_find_sub(fd, sender, inst)
        if existing >= 0
            reply[0] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val slot = unix_epoll_alloc_sub()
        if slot < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-UNIX_ENOMEM)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        unix_epoll_subs[slot].in_use = 1
        unix_epoll_subs[slot].unix_fd = fd
        unix_epoll_subs[slot].owner_tid = sender
        unix_epoll_subs[slot].epoll_inst = inst
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


    unix_handle_epoll_unsub(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val inst = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        var reply: [1]byte
        val slot = unix_epoll_find_sub(fd, sender, inst)
        if slot >= 0
            unix_epoll_subs[slot].in_use = 0
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


    unix_handle_epoll_inst_close(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val inst = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        unix_epoll_drop_inst(sender, inst)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### CLOSE handler

Frees the slot. If the slot has a STREAM peer, marks the
peer as `peer_closed` and unparks any blocked recv there
with a 0-byte reply (clean EOF). Listener cleanup walks
the backlog and forcibly closes pending server-end slots.

    unix_handle_close(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var reply: [1]byte
            reply[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return

Drop the caller from the owners ring. If other owners or
in-flight queue references remain, the slot stays alive —
just reply success without touching peers/backlog/parked
state.

        unix_slot_remove_owner(fd, sender)
        if unix_sockets[fd].owners_count > 0
            var reply: [1]byte
            reply[0] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].nrefs_in_queues > 0
            var reply: [1]byte
            reply[0] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return

Last owner gone and no queue refs — run the full peer-EOF /
listener-flush / parked-accept-EBADF cleanup, then free.

        unix_full_close(fd)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### GETSOCKNAME handler

Returns the slot's `bound_path` and `path_len`. Unbound slots
report `plen=0`. The shim translates that into Linux's
`addrlen=2` convention (just the family field) on the user
side. Accept-allocated server ends carry the listener's
bound_path (copied at accept time) so this returns the
listening socket's path, matching Linux semantics.

Wire format:
  Request:  cmd(1) + fd(1) = 2 bytes
  Reply:    status(1) + plen(1) + path[plen] = 2 + plen bytes
            (or status(1) alone on validation failure)

    unix_handle_getsockname(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        val plen = unix_sockets[fd].path_len
        var reply: [128]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte(plen & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < plen
            reply[2 + i] = unix_sockets[fd].bound_path[i]
            i += 1
        ipc_reply(sender, &reply[0], 2 + plen)


### GETPEERNAME handler

For STREAM with `peer_idx >= 0`: returns the peer's
`bound_path`. The accept-allocated server end's peer is the
connector, whose bound_path is empty unless the client
explicitly bound itself — fall back to the cached
`connector_path` so apps that bound the client see its name
and apps that didn't see plen=0.

For STREAM with `peer_idx < 0` (never connected, or peer
already gone): -ENOTCONN. We don't try to detect "peer was
connected then died" precisely — once peer_idx is cleared by
peer's full_close, the connection is gone and -ENOTCONN is
the right Linux answer.

DGRAM: -ENOTCONN unconditionally. SLIX AF_UNIX has no DGRAM
`connect()` to remember a peer.

Wire format:
  Request:  cmd(1) + fd(1) = 2 bytes
  Reply:    status(1) + plen(1) + path[plen] = 2 + plen bytes
            (or status(1) alone on validation failure)

    unix_handle_getpeername(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if unix_sockets[fd].stype != UNIX_SOCK_STREAM
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ENOTCONN)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        val peer = unix_sockets[fd].peer_idx
        if peer < 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ENOTCONN)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        var plen = unix_sockets[peer].path_len
        var src: [108]byte
        var i = 0
        while i < plen
            src[i] = unix_sockets[peer].bound_path[i]
            i += 1
        if plen == 0
            val cplen = unix_sockets[fd].connector_path_len
            i = 0
            while i < cplen
                src[i] = unix_sockets[fd].connector_path[i]
                i += 1
            plen = cplen
        var reply: [128]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte(plen & 0xff)
        i = 0
        while i < plen
            reply[2 + i] = src[i]
            i += 1
        ipc_reply(sender, &reply[0], 2 + plen)


### SOCKETPAIR handler

Linux's `socketpair(AF_UNIX, SOCK_STREAM|SOCK_DGRAM, 0, sv)`
returns a pair of pre-connected fds. We allocate two fresh
slots owned by the caller and link them via `peer_idx`. For
STREAM that's exactly the post-accept topology the existing
send/recv handlers already understand. For DGRAM, the SENDTO
handler treats `plen == 0` as "default to peer_idx", so a
write/send on either side routes a datagram into the other's
`dg_queue` without a path lookup.

Wire format. Request: `cmd(1) + stype(1)` = 2 bytes.
Reply: `status(1) + fd0(1) + fd1(1)` = 3 bytes (or
`status(1)` alone on validation/allocation failure).

    unix_handle_socketpair(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val stype = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if stype != UNIX_SOCK_STREAM
            if stype != UNIX_SOCK_DGRAM
                var err: [1]byte
                err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
                ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
                return
        val fd0 = unix_alloc_slot(sender, stype)
        if fd0 < 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ENOMEM)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        val fd1 = unix_alloc_slot(sender, stype)
        if fd1 < 0
            unix_free_slot(fd0)
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_ENOMEM)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        unix_sockets[fd0].peer_idx = fd1
        unix_sockets[fd1].peer_idx = fd0
        var reply: [3]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte(fd0 & 0xff)
        reply[2] = byte(fd1 & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, &reply[0], 3)


### SHUTDOWN handler

Linux `shutdown(fd, how)` semantics on AF_UNIX SOCK_STREAM:
SHUT_RD (0) silences future reads on this fd (returns 0 EOF
without consuming `rx_buf` data — Linux preserves the buffer);
SHUT_WR (1) signals end-of-stream to the peer so its reads
drain `rx_buf` and then return 0 EOF, while writes on this fd
return -EPIPE; SHUT_RDWR (2) is both. DGRAM is treated as a
no-op (matches Linux + the existing UDP path in
`sys_shutdown`).

We bump `fire_seq` on both this fd and the peer so any
epoll/poll watcher wakes up to observe the new EOF/HUP edge.
Any reader parked on the peer is unblocked with a zero-byte
EOF reply for SHUT_WR; any reader parked locally is unblocked
for SHUT_RD.

Wire format. Request: `cmd(1) + fd(1) + how(1)` = 3 bytes.
Reply: `status(1)` (0 on success, errno byte on failure).

    unix_handle_shutdown(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val how = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        if unix_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EBADF)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if how != 0
            if how != 1
                if how != 2
                    var err: [1]byte
                    err[0] = byte(-UNIX_EINVAL)
                    ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
                    return
        if unix_sockets[fd].stype != UNIX_SOCK_STREAM
            // DGRAM no-op — matches sys_shutdown's UDP handling.
            var ok: [1]byte
            ok[0] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, ok, 1)
            return
        val want_rd = if how == 0 then 1 else if how == 2 then 1 else 0
        val want_wr = if how == 1 then 1 else if how == 2 then 1 else 0
        if want_rd != 0
            unix_sockets[fd].shut_rd = 1
        if want_wr != 0
            unix_sockets[fd].shut_wr = 1
        unix_bump_fire_seq(fd)
        val peer = unix_sockets[fd].peer_idx
        if peer >= 0
            unix_bump_fire_seq(peer)

If we just shut our write side, anyone parked reading on the
peer gets an EOF reply now — its `rx_buf` is already empty
(otherwise it wouldn't be parked) and our `shut_wr` is what
the post-buf path now checks.

        if want_wr != 0
            if peer >= 0
                val parked = unix_sockets[peer].pending_recv_tid
                if parked >= 0
                    unix_sockets[peer].pending_recv_tid = -1
                    var pr: [3]byte
                    pr[0] = 0
                    unix_write_u16(&pr[0], 1, 0)
                    ipc_reply(parked, &pr[0], 3)

Symmetric: a SHUT_RD with a local parker means the parker
should also wake — its next recv will see `shut_rd` and return
0. (Rare but possible if the caller raced a self-shutdown
with an in-flight blocking recv.)

        if want_rd != 0
            val self_parked = unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_tid
            if self_parked >= 0
                unix_sockets[fd].pending_recv_tid = -1
                var pr: [3]byte
                pr[0] = 0
                unix_write_u16(&pr[0], 1, 0)
                ipc_reply(self_parked, &pr[0], 3)

        var ok: [1]byte
        ok[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, ok, 1)


### PID_EXIT handler

PM calls this when a process exits, with the dead thread's
TID. We sweep slot-owner-by-tid, mirroring inet's
pid_exit handler. Mostly harmless if a malformed sender
passes a foreign tid — they don't own those slots so the
sweep is a no-op against everything else.

    unix_handle_pid_exit(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val tid = unix_read_u32(buf, 1)
        unix_epoll_drop_owner(tid)
        var i = 0
        while i < UNIX_MAX_SOCKETS
            if unix_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if unix_sockets[i].pending_recv_tid == tid
                    unix_sockets[i].pending_recv_tid = -1
                    unix_sockets[i].pending_recv_is_msg = 0
                if unix_sockets[i].pending_accept_tid == tid
                    unix_sockets[i].pending_accept_tid = -1
                if unix_slot_has_owner(i, tid) != 0
                    unix_slot_remove_owner(i, tid)
                    if unix_sockets[i].owners_count == 0
                        if unix_sockets[i].nrefs_in_queues == 0
                            unix_full_close(i)
            i += 1
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### Server main loop

Synchronous request-reply, no notification path required
(no NIC, no timer subscription). One slot per IPC message.

    unix_server()
        unix_init_sockets()
        unix_epoll_init_subs()
        unix_port = port_create()
        port_register(unix_port, "unix")
        rs_ready()

        var buf: [512]byte
        while 1 == 1
            val sender = ipc_recv(unix_port, buf, UNIX_MSG_BUF)
            if sender >= 0
                val cmd = int(buf[0]) & 0xff
                if cmd == UNIX_CMD_SOCKET
                    unix_handle_socket(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_BIND
                    unix_handle_bind(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_CONNECT
                    unix_handle_connect(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_LISTEN
                    unix_handle_listen(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_ACCEPT
                    unix_handle_accept(sender, buf, 0)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_SENDTO
                    unix_handle_sendto(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_RECVFROM
                    unix_handle_recvfrom(sender, buf, 0)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_SEND
                    unix_handle_send(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_RECV
                    unix_handle_recv(sender, buf, 0)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_CLOSE
                    unix_handle_close(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_PID_EXIT
                    unix_handle_pid_exit(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_SENDMSG
                    unix_handle_sendmsg(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_RECVMSG
                    unix_handle_recvmsg(sender, buf, 0)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_RECVMSG_NB
                    unix_handle_recvmsg(sender, buf, 1)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_ACCEPT_NB
                    unix_handle_accept(sender, buf, 1)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_RECVFROM_NB
                    unix_handle_recvfrom(sender, buf, 1)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_RECV_NB
                    unix_handle_recv(sender, buf, 1)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_POLL
                    unix_handle_poll(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_EPOLL_SUB
                    unix_handle_epoll_sub(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB
                    unix_handle_epoll_unsub(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE
                    unix_handle_epoll_inst_close(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_GETSOCKNAME
                    unix_handle_getsockname(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_GETPEERNAME
                    unix_handle_getpeername(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_SOCKETPAIR
                    unix_handle_socketpair(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == UNIX_CMD_SHUTDOWN
                    unix_handle_shutdown(sender, buf)
                else
                    var reply: [1]byte
                    reply[0] = byte(-1)
                    ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
